    "summary": "Alabama shows widespread resistance, with 23 moratoria logged and most scored counties rated high. The strongest cases are Morgan County (county and city moratoria, with Somerville and Trinity also pausing), Baldwin County (a 180-day county moratorium plus Foley), Jefferson County (Homewood, Pinson, Tarrant and Birmingham pauses, plus the Bessemer backlash that unseated two councilmembers) and Shelby County (Chelsea, Helena and Westover moratoria). Lowndes County stands out for organized opposition and two lawsuits despite a 3-2 commission vote granting tax abatements, while Tuscaloosa County is the clearest counterexample, with approvals and abatements moving forward, and Chilton County rescinded its moratorium; many other counties score high mainly on a county-wide base rate with little local evidence."
