    "summary": "Texas shows the strongest documented resistance in a cluster of counties that have formally acted: Hays (5-0 suspension of discretionary approvals and incentives after San Marcos rejected the 380 MW Highlander project), Denton (unanimous move toward a moratorium, with Prosper's Project Tomahawk withdrawn), Cameron (Brownsville and Harlingen moratoria), Austin County (a countywide moratorium on AI data centers and battery storage), and Lubbock (a 5-2 vote to begin a 90-day moratorium after a 3,900-signature petition). Fort Worth in Tarrant County, Caldwell, Somervell, Henderson, Hood and Archer counties also stand out for moratoria, incentive suspensions, withdrawn projects or denied permits, though Texas counties' limited zoning power and the Hill County lawsuit that forced a moratorium's rescission temper what they can enforce. Many other counties score high mainly on a county-wide base rate with little local evidence, while places like Williamson, Young, Carson, Armstrong, Pecos and Nolan show approvals despite organized opposition."
  },
